2.2. Tracking performance

For each participant for each tracking task we calculated the mean square error (MSE) tracking displacement in pixels (see Table 1 for the mean MSE for each condition) We utilized two pre-planned orthogonal statistical contrasts for testing differences in mean square tracking error ( Hays, 1973 ; Schad et al., 2020 ). The first test was between the tracking single-task with the average of the two tracking with word recall tasks, both no warning signal and warning signal conditions combined. Participants had significantly less tracking error in the tracking alone condition (M = 272.5, SD = 166.1) than in the tracking dual-task conditions (M = 381.0, SD = 196.6), t(17) = 4.39, p < .001, M difference  = 108.5, 95%CI [56.3, 160.7], Cohen's d = 1.03. For the second test, there was no significant difference between dual-task tracking error without a warning signal (M = 374.8, SD = 224.4) and dual-task tracking error with a warning signal (M = 387.3, SD = 204.0), t(17) = 0.31, p = .761, M difference  = 12.5, 95%CI [−72.7, 97.7], Cohen's d = 0.07.
2.3. Post-event tracking performance: Finer grain analysis

Three types of events occurred during the tracking conditions in the experiment: word presentations on the HMD, drag force changes of drag which was being applied to the cursor, and paired word presentations on the HMD with drag force changes. Because the paired presentations created drag force changes under slightly different rules than the regular drag force changes, artificial “word” presentation times were generated for the tracking alone condition even though the HMD actually remained blank. Each participant had between nine and eleven word presentations, twenty four and thirty one drag force changes, and nine and eleven paired presentations. Fig. 3 , Fig. 4 , Fig. 5 display the average post-event effects on participants’ tracking.
